---
title: "Sort Algorithms: Choosing a Strategy from stdlib/sort"
author: celaltokmak61
date: 2026-11-27
abstract: A practical tour of stdlib/sort.ltl — quicksort, mergesort, insertion sort, selection sort, heapsort, and the keyed helper — with pinned vectors you can run with lateralus test.
---

## The pitch

Sorting looks solved until you need a stable order, a keyed order, or a nearly-sorted list. `stdlib/sort.ltl` puts five strategies behind one small API so you can change the strategy without rewriting the call site.

```lateralus
import sort

let values = [3, 1, 2, 1]
println(sort.sorted(values))
println(sort.sorted_desc(values))
```

```
[1, 1, 2, 3]
[3, 2, 1]
```

## The API surface

| Function | Use it when | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `sorted(items)` | Default ascending order | Delegates to three-way quicksort |
| `sorted_desc(items)` | Reverse order | Quicksort then `reverse` |
| `sort_by_key(items, key_fn)` | Records, pairs, objects | Decorate-sort-undecorate |
| `mergesort(items)` | Equal keys must keep input order | Stable |
| `insertion_sort(items)` | Small or nearly-sorted lists | O(n^2) worst case |
| `selection_sort(items)` | Minimal writes matter | O(n^2), few swaps |
| `is_sorted(items)` | Cheap precondition check | O(n) |

## Quicksort is the default for a reason

`quicksort` picks the middle element as a pivot, partitions into `<`, `==`, and `>` buckets, and recurses. The three-way split is what makes duplicate-heavy input cheap: every `1` in `[3, 1, 2, 1]` lands in `mid` once instead of being recursively compared.

Pinned vector:

```lateralus
@test
fn sorted_handles_duplicates() -> bool {
    return sort.sorted([3, 1, 2, 1]) == [1, 1, 2, 3]
}
```

## Stability is a separate promise

Quicksort may reorder equal keys. Mergesort does not. If you sort rows by a priority field and need same-priority rows to keep their original order, reach for `mergesort`.

Pinned vector:

```lateralus
@test
fn mergesort_is_stable() -> bool {
    let rows = [[2, "b"], [1, "a"], [2, "c"]]
    let expected = [[1, "a"], [2, "b"], [2, "c"]]
    return sort.mergesort(rows) == expected
}
```

## Sorting by a key

`sort_by_key` decorates each item with its key, quicksorts the pairs, then strips the decoration. It is the right tool when the sort criterion is not the value itself.

Pinned vector:

```lateralus
@test
fn sort_by_key_orders_pairs() -> bool {
    let rows = [[3, "c"], [1, "a"], [2, "b"]]
    let got = sort.sort_by_key(rows, fn(row) { return row[0] })
    return got == [[1, "a"], [2, "b"], [3, "c"]]
}
```

## Nearly-sorted input

`insertion_sort` walks the list once and inserts each element into the already-sorted prefix. On a list that is already sorted, each inner loop exits immediately.

Pinned vector:

```lateralus
@test
fn insertion_sort_keeps_nearly_sorted_input() -> bool {
    return sort.insertion_sort([1, 2, 3, 5, 4]) == [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
}
```

## Checking before sorting

`is_sorted` is useful as a guard: if the input is already ordered, skip the sort entirely.

Pinned vectors:

```lateralus
@test
fn is_sorted_accepts_duplicates() -> bool {
    return sort.is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 3])
}

@test
fn is_sorted_rejects_inversion() -> bool {
    return sort.is_sorted([2, 1]) == false
}
```

## Runnable example

Save this as `examples/sort_tutorial.ltl` and run `lateralus test examples/sort_tutorial.ltl`.

```lateralus
import sort

@test
fn sorted_handles_duplicates() -> bool {
    return sort.sorted([3, 1, 2, 1]) == [1, 1, 2, 3]
}

@test
fn sorted_desc_reverses() -> bool {
    return sort.sorted_desc([3, 1, 2]) == [3, 2, 1]
}

@test
fn mergesort_is_stable() -> bool {
    let rows = [[2, "b"], [1, "a"], [2, "c"]]
    return sort.mergesort(rows) == [[1, "a"], [2, "b"], [2, "c"]]
}

@test
fn sort_by_key_orders_pairs() -> bool {
    let rows = [[3, "c"], [1, "a"], [2, "b"]]
    let got = sort.sort_by_key(rows, fn(row) { return row[0] })
    return got == [[1, "a"], [2, "b"], [3, "c"]]
}

@test
fn insertion_sort_keeps_nearly_sorted_input() -> bool {
    return sort.insertion_sort([1, 2, 3, 5, 4]) == [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
}

@test
fn is_sorted_checks_guards() -> bool {
    return sort.is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 3]) && !sort.is_sorted([2, 1])
}
```

Expected output:

```
6/6 tests passed
```

## Choosing quickly

- Unknown shape: `sorted`.
- Equal keys with meaningful input order: `mergesort`.
- Key is a field: `sort_by_key`.
- Already almost ordered: `insertion_sort`.
- Want to short-circuit: check `is_sorted` first.

Sorting is not one algorithm. It is one API with the right strategy behind it.
